  "editorial": {
    "get_started": "Most people run Homepage with Docker Compose. You mount a local config folder into the container, expose port 3000, and then place it behind your normal reverse proxy if you want it on a private domain. The official Docker docs also show Docker run, Kubernetes, Unraid, and source install paths.\n\nBefore using it seriously, decide where your config files live and whether you want Docker socket access for automatic service discovery. Docker socket access should be read-only and treated carefully, because it lets the dashboard inspect container metadata.",
    "verdict": "Homepage is a strong fit when you want one polished launch page for a homelab without adding a database or account system. The main tradeoff is that most of the power comes from YAML config, so it rewards people who are comfortable keeping service links, widgets, and secrets organized in files.",
    "good_fit": [
      "Homelab start pages",
      "Dashboards for Docker services and status widgets",
      "Users who prefer config files over an admin UI"
    ],
